## Context

Before committing the paper's argument, stress-test it against
independent LLM critics. Different models surface different
weaknesses — training-distribution biases, methodological priors,
domain familiarity. Cheap insurance against blind spots.

The audit also doubles as a calibration probe: if frontier models
converge on the same critique, that signal is stronger than any
single reviewer's voice.

## Actions

1. Define an audit prompt:
   > Read `docs/argument.md`. Identify (a) the strongest internal
   > inconsistency, (b) the weakest empirical claim, (c) the most
   > likely methodological objection from a peer reviewer, (d) the
   > one claim you would drop if forced. Cite specific lines. Be
   > terse.
2. Run on a model panel: GPT-5.5, Claude Opus 4.7, Gemini 2.5 Pro,
   Mistral Large / Magistral, DeepSeek V3.2, Qwen3-Max-Thinking.
   Use deep-research / reasoning surfaces where available so the
   model can do its own homework on cited refs.
3. Aggregate findings: cluster by which claim each model contests.
   Distinct objections go in a critique table; consensus
   objections go top.
4. Either revise `argument.md` to address top critiques, or
   document each objection as "considered and rejected because X".

## Exit criteria

- Audit run on ≥5 frontier models.
- Aggregated critique table committed as
  `docs/argument-audit.md`.
- Each top-cluster objection either resolved in `argument.md` or
  flagged with an explicit justification for keeping the claim.
- Audit prompt and model versions logged for reproducibility.
